... Respondent\(s\)

STATUTES & ACTS REFERRED:
Prevention of Corruption Act 1988, Indian Evidence Act 1872

SECTIONS & PROVISIONS:
PC Act Section 7, PC Act Section 13\(1\)\(d\), IEA Section 3

KEY TOPICS & ISSUES:
Prevention of Corruption, Demand and Acceptance, Trap Witness, Circumstantial Proof, Shadow Witness

================================================================================
                                   JUDGMENT
================================================================================

1. FACTS OF THE CASE:
Clarification sought on whether demand and acceptance in bribery cases can be inferred purely on shadow witness testimony without complainant.

2. ISSUES FRAMED:
What standard of circumstantial evidence suffices to prove demand and acceptance when primary complainant is hostile or deceased?

3. LEGAL RATIO & FINDINGS:
Demand and acceptance of illegal gratification can be proved by circumstantial evidence, but evidence must lead to sole inference of guilt.

4. FINAL ORDER:
Review Clarified; Corroboration of demand and acceptance through circumstantial evidence must satisfy chain of proof.

Ordered and decreed accordingly.

Hon'ble Constitution Bench \(5 Judges\)
Supreme Court of India
